{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Conceptual Model.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Conceptual Model.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"conceptual model conceptual model","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Conceptual Model.md","fileName":"Conceptual Model.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Conceptual Model","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 13:11\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Human-Computer Interaction Foundations]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Human-Computer Interaction Foundations","Usability","Feedback","Affordance","Game Model","Player Mental Model","3duserinterfaces2ande.pdf","advancedgamedesign.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Conceptual Model.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Conceptual Model.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"conceptual model conceptual model","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Conceptual Model.md","fileName":"Conceptual Model.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Conceptual Model","heading":"Conceptual Model","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A conceptual model is a person's understanding of the information and tasks that make up a system. It can be informal and incomplete because it represents an understanding rather than the system itself.\n\nThe designer's model is the relatively complete view formed during development and may include task analyses, diagrams, and implementation knowledge. The user's model is built from experience with the interface. For high [[Usability]], the system should communicate the designer's model clearly enough that the user's model becomes accurate.\n\nConsistent behavior, visible system state, [[Feedback]], and [[Affordance]]s help align the two models.\n\nGames make this alignment dynamic: the [[Game Model]] changes in response to action, while the [[Player Mental Model]] changes in response to feedback. Productive uncertainty leaves room for prediction and learning without making the game's effects seem arbitrary.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Human-Computer Interaction Foundations","Usability","Feedback","Affordance","Game Model","Player Mental Model","3duserinterfaces2ande.pdf","advancedgamedesign.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Conceptual Model.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Conceptual Model.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"conceptual model conceptual model","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Conceptual Model.md","fileName":"Conceptual Model.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Conceptual Model","heading":"References","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"[[3duserinterfaces2ande.pdf]]\n[[advancedgamedesign.pdf]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Human-Computer Interaction Foundations","Usability","Feedback","Affordance","Game Model","Player Mental Model","3duserinterfaces2ande.pdf","advancedgamedesign.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""}]}